roblem 13](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_4/13).\n\nUse $y''+9y=\\cos(3x)+\\sin(3x)$." 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754324 1086 #uuid "717d7c8a-7c96-5e3f-9eed-e2dd13a6157c" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754324 1087 #edb/ref 17592186754389 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754325 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754264 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754325 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754325 1077 "solution" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754325 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754264 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754325 1079 #uuid "721c16ba-f90d-5eaf-a439-8b774f83341c" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754326 1080 "\\frac{x^{2} e^{4 x}}{2} + \\left(c_1 + c_2 x\\right) e^{4 x}" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754326 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754326 1083 #uuid "7536c0ef-25cd-5849-8539-7b1bb563af0a" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754415 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754327 1071 "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 8](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_4/8).\n\nFind $y(x)$ satisfying $x^2y''+5xy'-21y=0$, $y(1)=-9$, and $y'(1)=-3$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{solution}}." 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754327 1072 #uuid "7628824f-381b-584a-ad74-c603fca83c01" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754327 1074 "Use $y=x^r$. The indicial equation is $r(r-1)+5r-21=(r-3)(r+7)=0$, so $y=Ax^3+Bx^{-7}$. The conditions give $A+B=-9$ and $3A-7B=-3$, hence $A=-33/5$, $B=-12/5$. The maximal nonsingular interval containing $x=1$ is $x>0$." 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754403 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754328 1071 "**(b)** Find the solution satisfying $y(0)=5$ and $y'(0)=0$.\n\nEquation: $y=$ {{solution