 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754369 1062 #uuid "c0053602-e8f9-5842-9ca9-8fe9df219f6a" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754369 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754331 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754370 1080 "- 2 x + \\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{3 e^{- x}}{2}" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754370 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754370 1083 #uuid "c1ddbc66-4ccb-5a7f-995c-3922a93e8d26" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754275 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754371 1071 "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 16](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_4/16).\n\nUse the method of undetermined coefficients to find one solution of $y''+3y'-3y=(-t^2+9t+5)e^{3t}$. The method is to produce a specific solution, not the general solution.\n\n$y(t)=$ {{solution}}." 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754371 1072 #uuid "c25b0f5b-f78e-5dbb-9097-b19d9a3fdbcb" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754371 1074 "Use the undetermined-coefficients trial $y=e^{3t}(At^2+Bt+C)$. Put $v=At^2+Bt+C$; substitution reduces the left side to $e^{3t}(v''+9v'+15v)$. Matching coefficients gives $15A=-1$, $18A+15B=9$, and $2A+9B+15C=5$. Therefore $A=-1/15$, $B=17/25$, $C=-74/1125$, so $y=e^{3t}(-t^2/15+17t/25-74/1125)$. Direct differentiation verifies the original equation." 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754374 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754372 1071 "Find the solution to the original nonhomogeneous equation satisfying $y(0)=6$ and $y'(0)=3$.\n\n$y=$ {{solution}}." 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754372 1072 #uuid "c537203a-3a2e-5b3f-b901-0a3c361f3090" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754372 1074 "Using the particular solution $y_p=x(\\sin(3x)-\\cos(3x))/6$, we have $y_p(0)=0$ and $y_p'(0)=-1/6$. For $y=A\\cos(3x)+B\\sin(3x)+y_p$, the conditions give $A=6$ and $3B-1/6=3$, hence $B=19/18$." 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754373 1062 #uuid "c5a816a7-5d32-5594-838b-b2439d70eeb6" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754373 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754409 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754304 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754374 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754374 1077 "solution" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754374 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754304 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754374 1079 #uuid "c9558970-7b7f-5f74-9c1e-a64d33983864" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754300 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754375 1071 "Find the most general solution to the original nonhomogeneous equation. Use $c_1,c_2$ for arbitrary constants.\n\n$y=$ {{solution}}." 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754375 1072 #uuid "c9a45dfc-743a-532d-952a-320e6b25755b" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754375 1074 "Add a particular solution to the complete homogeneous solution: $y=y_h+y_p$. For the polynomial forcing use $Ax+B$: $5A=-10$, $4A+5B=0$, giving $A=-2$, $B=8/5$. For $3e^{-x}$ use $Ce^{-x}$: $(1-4+5)C=3$, giving $C=3/2$. Add the two particular solutions by linearity. The characteristic polynomial is $(r+2)^2+1$, so the roots $-2\\pm i$ give $y_h=e^{-2x}(c_1\\cos x+c_2\\sin x)$." 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754376 1080 "- \\frac{33 x^{3}}{5} - \\frac{12}{5 x^{7}}" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754376 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754376 1083 #uuid "ccd5fb62-a481-57b6-bbc4-de0525339c5d" 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754377 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754270 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754377 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139546555 17592186502323 true] [17592186754377 1077 "solution" 13194139546555 175